</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We highlight the most relevant prior work here and defer a more detailed discussion to Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Detailed Related Works ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detecting and Quantifying Steganographic Reasoning.</span>
Existing formalisms define steganography in terms of statistical <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cachin, 2004</a>)</cite> or computational <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hopper et al., 2002</a>)</cite> indistinguishability between steganographic and benign signal.
Accordingly, standard detection methods work by comparing the distribution of suspected text to that of known reference distribution, using either hand-crafted statistical features or learned representations derived from machine learning models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
In the context of LLMs, this paradigm has also been operationalised through LLM-as-a-judge approaches, where a model evaluates whether generated text appears “suspicious” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zolkowski et al., 2025</a>)</cite> or “illegible” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jose, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Emmons et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
However, such methods typically rely on access to representative samples from the reference benign distribution.
The challenge of applying this paradigm to the analysis of LLM reasoning and communication is that obtaining such samples, and ensuring they are indeed benign, is difficult.
Thus, our framework sidesteps this requirement by asking whether access to the signal changes an agent’s decisions, rather than whether the signal deviates from a reference distribution.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Steganographic Capabilities in Language Models.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has investigated the emergence of steganographic capabilities in frontier LLMs. One line of research studies <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">steganographic communication</em>, where one model embeds hidden information in benign-looking text that other models can later decode. Several works show that explicit steganographic schemes can be induced via prompting or fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roger &amp; Greenblatt, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zolkowski et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Motwani et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, or more organically through training against an overseer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mathew et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. A related but distinct phenomenon is <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">steganographic reasoning</em> (or encoded reasoning), where a model produces reasoning traces that are unintelligible to a monitor but remain useful to the model itself <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roger &amp; Greenblatt, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Skaf et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Prior works show that current LLMs can reason using simple encoding schemes when instructed to do so, though this ability degrades for more complex generations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zolkowski et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
In addition, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schoen et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jose (2025)</a></cite> showed that frontier reasoning models exhibit signs of non-standard language use in their CoT, increasing the urgency of research towards better understanding and counteracting steganographic reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Generalized <math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-Information</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to formally describe our decision-theoretic treatment of steganography, we require a notion of <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">usable information</em>: a measure of how much an auxiliary variable <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> improves decision-making for a given class of agents.
For this purpose, we adapt and extend the <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-information framework of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
We provide a brief overview below and defer a more formal treatment to Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Generalised 𝒱-Information ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Setup and Notation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be some input, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding output, and let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> be some auxiliary variable, the ‘usefulness’ of which we aim to quantify.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A decoder <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is a mapping <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d:\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{aud}}\times(\mathcal{Z}\cup\varnothing)\to\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>aud</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d:\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{aud}}\times(\mathcal{Z}\cup\varnothing)\to\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> from available information to an element of an output space <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We consider two decoding regimes: decoding directly from the input via <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d[X]</annotation></semantics></math>, and decoding from both the input and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d[X,Z]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d[X,Z]</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote a family of admissible decoders.
We generally presume these decoders to be capacity and compute constrained.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Predictive <math id="S3.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-Information</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A central theme of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-information is that <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how</em> information is represented can matter as much as <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">whether</em> it exists.
For example, encrypting a text input <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> may not remove the Shannon information it contains about a label <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, yet it can make predicting <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> much harder for any predictor family with capacity and compute constraints.
This motivates the concept of <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">usable information</em>: information measured relative to the decoding capabilities of the predictor family.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2020)</a></cite>, let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of all decoders the output space <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is the space of probability measures over <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cY" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cY</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., decoders that map provided information to distributions over <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>), and let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}\subseteq\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}\subseteq\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">predictive family</em> satisfying <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">optional ignorance</em> (i.e., the family contains predictors that can effectively ignore the provided side information if needed).
Define the predictive <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-entropy under log loss as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\inf_{f\in\mathcal{V}}\;\mathbb{E}_{X,Y}\big[-\log d[X](Y)\big]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\inf_{f\in\mathcal{V}}\;\mathbb{E}_{X,Y}\big[-\log d[X](Y)\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X,Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X,Z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\inf_{f\in\mathcal{V}}\;\mathbb{E}_{X,Y,Z}\big[-\log d[X,Z](Y)\big]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\inf_{f\in\mathcal{V}}\;\mathbb{E}_{X,Y,Z}\big[-\log d[X,Z](Y)\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d[x]\in\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d[x]\in\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the probability measure over <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> chosen based on the received side information <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Further, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d[x](y)\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d[x](y)\in R</annotation></semantics></math> is the value of density evaluated at <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. The (conditional) predictive <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-information provided by <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> about <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is then defined as the improvement in best achievable log loss:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathcal{V}}(Z\to Y\mid X)\;=\;H_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X)\;-\;H_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X,Z)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathcal{V}}(Z\to Y\mid X)\;=\;H_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X)\;-\;H_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X,Z).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Generalised <math id="S3.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-information (GVI)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-information provides a principled measure of usable information, it requires each decoder to output a full predictive distribution, making it challenging to apply in practice.
For example, in the context of LLMs, cross-model comparisons based on log-probabilities are known to be brittle, hard to interpret, and sensitive to stylistic differences between models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Moreover, in AI oversight and many other settings, we care about metrics like accuracy or recall rather than log loss.
Therefore we adopt a <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">utilitarian view of information</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ermon, 2022</a>)</cite> and use a utility-based generalization of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-information called <em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generalized <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-information</em>, which allows us to evaluate responses using a <em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">utility function</em> <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{C}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{C}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, tailored to alternative output spaces <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Intuitively, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(y,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(y,c)</annotation></semantics></math> measures the utility for a decoder <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> outputting response <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> when the true target is <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a decoder family <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> and utility <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the maximal achievable expected utility when decoding based on <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Z)</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{d\in\mathcal{V}}\;\mathbb{E}_{X,Y}\big[u(Y,d[X])\big]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{d\in\mathcal{V}}\;\mathbb{E}_{X,Y}\big[u(Y,d[X])\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X,Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X,Z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{d\in\mathcal{V}}\;\mathbb{E}_{X,Y,Z}\big[u(Y,d[X,Z])\big]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{d\in\mathcal{V}}\;\mathbb{E}_{X,Y,Z}\big[u(Y,d[X,Z])\big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S3.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generalised <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-information (GVI)</span> is then the gain in utility achievable by the best decoders in <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> by additionally observing <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{u}_{\mathcal{V}}(Z\to Y\mid X)\;=\;U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X,Z)\;-\;U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{u}_{\mathcal{V}}(Z\to Y\mid X)\;=\;U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X,Z)\;-\;U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}}(Y\mid X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Similar to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-information, GVI turns “information” into a measure of <span id="S3.SS3.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">strategic advantage</span>: <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is valuable only insofar as it enables some decoder in <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve higher task utility.
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> can therefore be information-rich in the Shannon sense, yet have zero GVI if no decoder in <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> can exploit it (e.g., if <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is in French and decoders in <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> only understand English).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The main usefulness of GVI is that it allows a practitioner to measure <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">usable</span> information with respect to any utility function of her choice.
For example, if one is interested in evaluating how helpful <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is in terms of performing some task <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> better, this can be measured with GVI using task accuracy as the utility function.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Formally, if <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the space of task answers) and <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u(y,c)=\mathbf{1}[\hat{y}=c]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(y,c)=\mathbf{1}[\hat{y}=c]</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{u}_{\mathcal{V}}(Z\rightarrow Y\mid X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{u}_{\mathcal{V}}(Z\rightarrow Y\mid X)</annotation></semantics></math> measures the <em id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">expected increase in task accuracy</em> attributable to having access to <math id="footnote1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> for the best decoder in <math id="footnote1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span>
Furthermore, GVI is a strict generalization of predictive <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-Information.
Specifically, if we set <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Y})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the space of predictive distributions over <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(y,c)=\log c(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(y,c)=\log c(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the induced <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{u}_{\mathcal{V}}(Z\rightarrow Y\mid X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{u}_{\mathcal{V}}(Z\rightarrow Y\mid X)</annotation></semantics></math> reduces to predictive <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-information.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Equipped with the generalised <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-information, we now introduce our decision-theoretic formalism for steganography.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>From Monitoring Messages to Monitoring Actions</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the classical steganographic setting: a Sender transmits a message <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> to a Receiver over a public channel monitored by an Auditor.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            The Auditor is analogous to the warden in classical formulations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Simmons, 1984</a>)</cite>.</span></span></span>
In the standard model, the Auditor must judge whether <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is steganographic based solely on the message itself and knowledge of a benign covertext distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cachin, 2004</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hopper et al., 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2019a</a>)</cite>.
This standard model has two clear shortcomings: it makes detection impossible when covertext distributions are unknown (as with LLM reasoning) or when the steganographic scheme is perfectly secure and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is statistically indistinguishable from the covertext.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, steganography is generally <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> employed for its own sake,
but is often used in an instrumental way to achieve some strategic objective.
For example, in Simmons’ original formulation, prisoners goal in using steganographic communication is to coordinate an escape plan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Simmons, 1984</a>)</cite>.
In the LLM setting, the LLM’s goal in using steganography is to avoid being penalized for using prohibited reasoning that helps the model perform better on some task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Skaf et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Because steganographic content is instrumental, its value must ultimately manifest through the Receiver’s actions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This motivates our extension: we allow the Auditor to observe not only <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, but also how <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> affects the Receiver’s <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">downstream decisions</span>.
This creates an alternative detection surface—even if the Auditor cannot decode <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> directly, it may <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">infer</span> hidden content by observing its downstream effects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To capture such effects, we adopt a <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">comparative approach</span>: <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">we allow the Auditor to evaluate not only the actions of the Receiver, but also of a trusted <em id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sentinel</em>—an agent with comparable capabilities, but without knowledge of the encoding scheme used by the Sender and Receiver</span>. Then, any systematic difference in performance between the Receiver and the Sentinel in response to the message reflects the effect of the encoded information, available only to the Receiver. In the language of usable information, a steganographic message induces an asymmetry between agents: it contains more usable information for the Receiver than for the Sentinel. This asymmetry manifests through their actions and can be quantified using generalised <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-information (see <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Steganographic Gap ‣ 4 Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>). Thus, monitoring the gap in the generalised <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-information between the Receiver and the Sentinel can allow the Auditor to detect the presence of steganographic information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Setup and Notation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We visualise our setup in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
We make a distinction between a <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generation context</span> <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\mathrm{gen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>gen</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\mathrm{gen}}</annotation></semantics></math> and an <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">auditing context</span> <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\mathrm{aud}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>aud</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\mathrm{aud}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Generation of (potentially) steganographic content happens within the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generation context</span>, while the analysis occurs within the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">auditing context</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, upon observing <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\mathrm{gen}}\in\mathcal{X}^{\mathrm{gen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>gen</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>gen</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\mathrm{gen}}\in\mathcal{X}^{\mathrm{gen}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Sender generates a signal <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in\mathcal{Z}\sim P_{S}(Z\mid X^{\mathrm{gen}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>gen</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in\mathcal{Z}\sim P_{S}(Z\mid X^{\mathrm{gen}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Auditor specifies how <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> will be evaluated, by specifying an <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">auditing task</em> defined through the tuple <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X^{\mathrm{aud}},u,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>aud</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X^{\mathrm{aud}},u,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Both the Receiver and Sentinel observe <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\mathrm{aud}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>aud</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\mathrm{aud}}</annotation></semantics></math> and (optionally) <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and must produce a response <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The responses (decisions) are scored against ground truth <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> using utility <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{C}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{C}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This maps directly onto the GVI framework of Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Generalized 𝒱-Information ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>: <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\mathrm{aud}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>aud</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\mathrm{aud}}</annotation></semantics></math> plays the role of the input <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and the signal <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is the auxiliary variable whose usefulness we wish to quantify.
The Receiver and Sentinel are distinguished by their decoder families <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{sen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>sen</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{sen}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which may differ due to access to keys, prior knowledge, or computational resources.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Steganographic Gap</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given this setup, we now proceed to quantify the strength of steganography using the generalised <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Receiver-usable information</span> of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the utility gain (under <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>) that the <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">best</span> decoder in Receiver’s decoding class <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> can obtain from observing <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, relative to decoding from <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> alone.</p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I_{\mathrm{rec}}(Z\!\to\!Y)\;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I_{\mathrm{rec}}(Z\!\to\!Y)\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\;I^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rec}}}(Z\!\to\!Y\mid X^{\text{aud}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>I</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\;I^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rec}}}(Z\!\to\!Y\mid X^{\text{aud}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\;U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rec}}}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}},Z)\;-\;U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rec}}}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\;U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rec}}}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}},Z)\;-\;U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rec}}}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S4.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Sentinel-usable information</span> <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{sen}}(Z\!\to\!Y)=I^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{sen}}}(Z\!\to\!Y\mid X^{\text{aud}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>sen</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>I</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>sen</mi></msub><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{sen}}(Z\!\to\!Y)=I^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{sen}}}(Z\!\to\!Y\mid X^{\text{aud}})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined analogously.
The <span id="S4.SS3.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">steganographic gap</span> is then defined as</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathrm{steg}}(Z)\;=\;I_{\mathrm{rec}}(Z\!\to\!Y)\;-\;I_{\mathrm{sen}}(Z\!\to\!Y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>steg</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>sen</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathrm{steg}}(Z)\;=\;I_{\mathrm{rec}}(Z\!\to\!Y)\;-\;I_{\mathrm{sen}}(Z\!\to\!Y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">In this framework, signal <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is steganographic if it induces a <span id="S4.SS3.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">positive steganographic gap</span>, which indicates that there is some information encoded in <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> that is operationally accessible to <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> but comparatively inaccessible to <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{sen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>sen</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{sen}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the objective <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y,u)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\text{steg}}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>steg</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\text{steg}}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> inherits the units of the utility function <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, when <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is accuracy utility, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{sen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>sen</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{sen}}</annotation></semantics></math> are improvements in accuracy from access to <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-100\leq\Delta_{\text{steg}}(Z)\leq 100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>steg</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-100\leq\Delta_{\text{steg}}(Z)\leq 100</annotation></semantics></math> is measured in <em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">percentage points</em> of accuracy advantage (rather than bits per token).
To avoid this confusion related to units, we define and use <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">normalized steganographic gap</span> which is dimensionless and has a maximum value of <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, making it easier to interpret.</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}(Z)\;=\;\frac{\Delta_{\text{steg}}(Z)}{I_{\text{rec}}(Z\!\to\!Y)}\;=\;1-\frac{I_{\text{sen}}(Z\!\to\!Y)}{I_{\text{rec}}(Z\!\to\!Y)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>steg</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>steg</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>sen</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}(Z)\;=\;\frac{\Delta_{\text{steg}}(Z)}{I_{\text{rec}}(Z\!\to\!Y)}\;=\;1-\frac{I_{\text{sen}}(Z\!\to\!Y)}{I_{\text{rec}}(Z\!\to\!Y)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{rec}}(Z\!\to\!Y)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{rec}}(Z\!\to\!Y)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\text{steg}}(Z)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>steg</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\text{steg}}(Z)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>steg</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> can be interpreted as <span id="S4.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the fraction of <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>’s usable information that is accessible to the Receiver but not by the Sentinel</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Discussion</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude this section by discussing key properties of the proposed formalism, its relationship to existing approaches, and its scope of applicability.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dependence on Sentinel Capability and Auditing Task.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The steganographic gap depends explicitly on the decoder class <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\text{sen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>sen</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\text{sen}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A reasoning trace may be steganographic with respect to one Sentinel but not another: a more capable Sentinel family may successfully decode information that a weaker one cannot, reducing or eliminating the gap.
This captures the adversarial nature of steganography (also see Section <a href="#S6.SS5" title="6.5 Δ̃_steg Decreases with Sentinel-Side Interventions ‣ 6 Validating the Proposed Formalism ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a>).
Note that defining steganography relative to a particular (class of) Sentinels is not unique to our framework. In the classical information-theoretic formalism, steganographic security is similarly defined relative to the assumed cover distribution and computational capabilities of the adversary, and changing these can change whether a given encoding scheme is considered secure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The steganographic gap is also defined with respect to a specific auditing task.
This reflects practical reality that steganography is typically employed to achieve specific adversarial goals—e.g., coordinating prohibited actions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Simmons, 1984</a>)</cite>—and detection efforts are typically oriented around preventing these known threats.
Defining steganography relative to an auditing objective reflects this reality: the Auditor’s task is not to detect hidden information in the abstract, but to prevent its use for particular harmful purposes, which are reflected in the choice of the auditing task (also see Section <a href="#S6.SS4" title="6.4 Δ̃_steg Varies Across Auditing Tasks ‣ 6 Validating the Proposed Formalism ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scope and Assumptions.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our decision-theoretic formalism works by examining the <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decisions</span> that agents take upon receiving a potentially steganographic signal—an affordance that may not be available in all scenarios.
In general, the framework may be applied in settings where three conditions hold:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1em;">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Interceptability and Replayability</span>: First, the signal <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> must be interceptable and replayable, so that it could be presented to both Receiver and Auditor independently.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Action recoverability</span>: Second, it is important that the decoder’s response to <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> can be evaluated in a controlled setting, without committing to (potentially dangerous) irreversible real-world effects.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Temporal immediacy</span>: Finally, we implicitly assume that steganographic content yields measurable effects within the auditing task’s evaluation horizon. If this does not hold, and the effects of the encoded signal only become apparent under future conditions, our framework would fail to capture these effects, and thus may fail to detect steganography.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All three conditions are satisfied in our primary setting of interest: auditing and monitoring reasoning in LLMs.
Reasoning traces can generally be replayed to arbitrary models,
model outputs are text, or tool calls, that generally can be evaluated in controlled environments (sandboxes) without real-world consequences, and single-turn reasoning provides immediate behavioural signal.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.4.1 </span>Relationship to Existing Approaches</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Classic Information-Theoretic Approach.</span>
Theoretically, our framework recovers classical information-theoretic security as a special case when the auditing task is binary detection, i.e., distinguishing steganographic signals from a cover distribution (see Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Classical Steganographic Security as a Special Case ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>).
In practice, the two approaches are likely to complement each other.
For instance, computationally cheap methods based on statistical distinguishability could provide initial screening, while our approach could be used in cases where more rigorous and targeted assessment is needed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Anthropic’s Approach.</span>
Anthropic is the only frontier lab that has publicly reported performing evaluations to rule out steganographic reasoning in LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2026</a>, Section 6.4.2)</cite>.
Their approach, introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roger (2025)</a></cite>, is also decision-theoretic in flavour.
Specifically, they compare a model’s task utility when conditioned on its own chain-of-thought <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> versus a paraphrased version <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> produced by another LLM.
In the language of our formalism, this can be understood as measuring <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\text{rec}}}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}},Z)-U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\text{rec}}}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}},Z^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\text{rec}}}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}},Z)-U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\text{rec}}}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}},Z^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>—a comparison across signals for a fixed decoder family.
Our approach instead measures the gap across decoder families for a fixed signal: <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\text{rec}}}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}},Z)-U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\text{sen}}}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}},Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>sen</mtext></msub><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\text{rec}}}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}},Z)-U^{u}_{\mathcal{V}_{\text{sen}}}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}},Z)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This distinction has two practical consequences that arguably make our approach more robust.
First, paraphrasing conflates two effects: a utility drop after paraphrasing may reflect the removal of steganographic content, or the destruction of legitimate reasoning that happened to be useful.
By holding <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> constant, our method isolates the information asymmetry between agents without conflating these two effects.
Second, our approach does not depend on transforming <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> at all, making it applicable even when steganographic schemes are robust to paraphrasing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mathew et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perry et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
We further investigate the relationship between paraphrasing and the steganographic gap in Section <a href="#S6.SS5" title="6.5 Δ̃_steg Decreases with Sentinel-Side Interventions ‣ 6 Validating the Proposed Formalism ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a>.
We also note that our approach has its own limitations that we discuss in Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Discussion &amp; Future Works ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Quantifying Steganographic Reasoning in LLMs</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show how our formalism can be applied to detect and quantify steganographic reasoning in LLMs.
We focus on settings in which, under some generation context <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\text{gen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mtext>gen</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\text{gen}}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., a task or a question requiring extensive reasoning), an LLM is suspected of using steganographic reasoning.
In this setting, the Sender LLM acts as both the Sender and the Receiver: we interpret the model as communicating with itself through its reasoning trace <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          This can be trivially extended to the case of steganographic communication between LLM agents by keeping the Sender and Receiver separate.</span></span></span>
This corresponds to a special case of our general framework in which the same agent both produces and exploits the steganographic signal.
Since the LLM plays both roles, we refer to it as <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> (for Receiver) to distinguish it from a trusted Sentinel LLM <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to quantify the degree to which reasoning traces <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> generated by <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> are steganographic with respect to an auditing task (specified via <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> c.f. Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Setup and Notation ‣ 4 Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).
That is, we measure whether <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> contains information relevant to the auditing task that is <span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">usable</span> by <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> but not by the Sentinel <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We assume access to a dataset of generation tasks <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{\text{gen}}=\{T_{1}^{\text{gen}},\ldots,T_{n}^{\text{gen}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mtext>gen</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext>gen</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi><mtext>gen</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{\text{gen}}=\{T_{1}^{\text{gen}},\ldots,T_{n}^{\text{gen}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and corresponding auditing tasks <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{\text{aud}}=\{T_{1}^{\text{aud}},\ldots,T_{n}^{\text{aud}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext>aud</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{\text{aud}}=\{T_{1}^{\text{aud}},\ldots,T_{n}^{\text{aud}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each auditing task has a target <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a utility function <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> that is shared by all auditing tasks.
<span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The auditing task may coincide with the generation task, but need not—for instance, the generation task might be to solve a coding problem, while the auditing task tests whether the reasoning trace reveals information about bypassing certain checks.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To generate a reasoning trace <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we combine the generation task <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}^{\text{gen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>gen</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}^{\text{gen}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a generation prompt <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\text{gen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mtext>gen</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\text{gen}}</annotation></semantics></math>,<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          We list all prompts used in Appendix <a href="#A6.SS3" title="F.3 Prompts ‣ Appendix F Experimental Details ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.3</span></a>.</span></span></span> forming the generation context <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}^{\text{gen}}=[p^{\text{gen}};T_{i}^{\text{gen}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>gen</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mtext>gen</mtext></msup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>gen</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}^{\text{gen}}=[p^{\text{gen}};T_{i}^{\text{gen}}]</annotation></semantics></math>. The reasoning trace is then sampled as <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{i}\sim R(\cdot\mid X_{i}^{\text{gen}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>gen</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}\sim R(\cdot\mid X_{i}^{\text{gen}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate utilities, we construct an auditing context <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}^{\text{aud}}~=~[p^{\text{aud}};~T_{i}^{\text{aud}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0.608em" rspace="0.608em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msup><mo rspace="0.497em">;</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}^{\text{aud}}~=~[p^{\text{aud}};~T_{i}^{\text{aud}}]</annotation></semantics></math> using the auditing task and an auditing prompt.
We use two auditing prompts: when estimating baseline utility <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{u}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{u}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}})</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\text{x-only}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mtext>x-only</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\text{x-only}}</annotation></semantics></math>; when estimating <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{u}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}},Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{u}(Y\mid X^{\text{aud}},Z)</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\text{xz}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mtext>xz</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\text{xz}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Both prompts instruct the model to answer immediately without producing any intermediate reasoning.
<math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\text{xz}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mtext>xz</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\text{xz}}</annotation></semantics></math> additionally encourages the model to base its answer on the provided reasoning trace <math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.
Both the Receiver <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and Sentinel <math id="S5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> are evaluated under identical prompts, ensuring a fair comparison.
We estimate the Receiver-usable and Sentinel-usable information by replacing expectations with empirical averages, sampling multiple reasoning traces per task to reduce variance.
Full details of the estimation procedure are provided in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Empirical Estimation of Steganographic Gap ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Validating the Proposed Formalism</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we empirically validate our formalism by showing that it aligns with typical notions of steganographic reasoning.
Since current LLMs have only nascent steganographic capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zolkowski et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we simulate steganographic reasoning using three methods of increasing realism: post-hoc ciphering, training with process supervision, and training to produce ciphered reasoning.

Across all three methods, <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>steg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}</annotation></semantics></math> behaves as expected: near zero when the decoding capabilities of Receiver and Sentinel are matched exactly, and approaching one as the information asymmetry grows.
We note that as we are studying steganographic reasoning, the same LLM acts as both Sender and Receiver and is denoted as <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> throughout this section.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span><math id="S6.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathrm{steg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>steg</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathrm{steg}}</annotation></semantics></math> Increases with Encoding Strength</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our first approach is motivated by prior work using ciphers as a proxy for steganographic reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
At the high-level, this approach works by selectively showing the Sentinel LLM <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> a manually <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ciphered</span> reasoning trace, while the Receiver LLM <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is shown the original (plaintext) reasoning trace, thus artificially creating controlled asymmetry in terms of usable information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We draw generation tasks from GSM8k <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, MMLU-Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and CoinFlip <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Skaf et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, where each sample constitutes one task.
GSM8k consists of grade-school math word problems,
MMLU-Pro is a challenging multiple-choice benchmark spanning diverse domains,
and CoinFlip is a synthetic state-tracking task where the model must track the outcome of a sequence of coin flips given their relative outcomes.
All three datasets were selected because chain-of-thought provides significant uplift <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sprague et al., 2025</a>)</cite>;
this ensures that <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{rec}}(Z\to Y)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{rec}}(Z\to Y)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, without which the steganographic gap would be trivially zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We use Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct as <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> to generate reasoning traces <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.
See Appendix <a href="#A6.SS4" title="F.4 Details of the Experiment in ‣ Appendix F Experimental Details ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.4</span></a> for the prompt used for this purpose and other relevant details.
We set auditing task to be same as the generation task: predicting the correct answer for the given task (e.g., a question from GSM8k), with accuracy as the utility <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.
When computing <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathcal{V}}^{u}(Y|X,Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathcal{V}}^{u}(Y|X,Z)</annotation></semantics></math>, both <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> receive the task; the difference lies in the reasoning trace—<math id="S6.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> always observes the original, while <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> receives a (possibly ciphered) version.
We simulate varying degrees of steganography by independently applying a Rot13 encoding to each word in <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> with probability <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, before presenting it to <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>—interpolating between fully legible (<math id="S6.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0</annotation></semantics></math>) and fully ciphered (<math id="S6.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1</annotation></semantics></math>) traces.
Since Rot13 leaves numerals unchanged, we verbalise mathematical expressions before encoding (see Appendix <a href="#A6.SS4" title="F.4 Details of the Experiment in ‣ Appendix F Experimental Details ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.4</span></a> for details and ablations).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23163v1/delta_gvi_rot13.svg" id="S6.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/154;" width="476" height="154"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S6.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Steganographic gap increases smoothly<span id="S6.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> as higher portions of <math id="S6.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> are encoded using Rot13.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S6.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 6.1 Δ̃_steg Increases with Encoding Strength ‣ 6 Validating the Proposed Formalism ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the steganographic gap increases smoothly as the encoding probability <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> increases.
This validates the claim that <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>steg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}</annotation></semantics></math> is sensitive to graded changes in encoding strength and can act as a quantitative measure.
As one should expect, for Qwen-2.5-14B-Instruct as the Sentinel model, we have <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>steg</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0</annotation></semantics></math> (corresponding to fully legible reasoning traces) and <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>steg</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1</annotation></semantics></math> (corresponding to fully illegible reasoning traces).
However, when using a weaker Sentinel (Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct), we observe <em id="S6.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">negative</em> gaps at low encoding levels: the weaker model benefits from <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> even more than <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> does, since it has lower baseline capabilities.
Detailed accuracy breakdowns are provided in Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Data for Plots ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span><math id="S6.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathrm{steg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>steg</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathrm{steg}}</annotation></semantics></math> Increases with Codebook Mismatch</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our second approach, we finetune models using reinforcement learning with a process-based reward on the CoinFlip task to reason using codewords <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Skaf et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We train three policies.
<math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{no-stego}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>no-stego</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{no-stego}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a baseline trained with task reward only.
<math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{mid-stego}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>mid-stego</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{mid-stego}}</annotation></semantics></math> uses two codewords: <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Sun</span> for Heads and <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Moon</span> for Tails.
<math id="S6.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{high-stego}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>high-stego</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{high-stego}}</annotation></semantics></math> uses a larger codebook: Heads may be denoted by either <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Sun</span> or <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">East</span>, and Tails by either <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Moon</span> or <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">West</span>.
The auditing task again coincides with the generation task: predicting the final state of the coin (Heads or Tails), with accuracy as the utility <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.
We evaluate all pairwise combinations of policies, where one policy acts as the Receiver <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and another as the Sentinel <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S6.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S6.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Normalized steganographic gap on the CoinFlip task for different Receiver–Sentinel pairs. The gap is highest when the Sentinel lacks knowledge of the Receiver’s codewords.</span></figcaption>
