</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6.2 Δ̃_steg Increases with Codebook Mismatch ‣ 6 Validating the Proposed Formalism ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the results.
As expected, <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>steg</mtext></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> are identical policies.
When they differ, a clear gap emerges: <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}=0.92" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>steg</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.92</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}=0.92</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R{=}\pi_{\text{high-stego}},S{=}\pi_{\text{no-stego}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>high-stego</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>no-stego</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R{=}\pi_{\text{high-stego}},S{=}\pi_{\text{no-stego}})</annotation></semantics></math>, approaching the theoretical maximum.
Interestingly, the gap depends on more than codebook overlap.
Both <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{\text{high-stego}},\pi_{\text{mid-stego}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>high-stego</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>mid-stego</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{\text{high-stego}},\pi_{\text{mid-stego}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{\text{mid-stego}},\pi_{\text{no-stego}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>mid-stego</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>no-stego</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{\text{mid-stego}},\pi_{\text{no-stego}})</annotation></semantics></math> involve two unknown codewords for the Sentinel, yet yield very different gaps (<math id="S6.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.29" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.29</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.29</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.70</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.70</annotation></semantics></math>).
This motivates treating Sentinel strength as an empirical property captured by <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{sen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>sen</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{sen}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as opposed to some simple statistic like codebook overlap.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span><math id="S6.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathrm{steg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>steg</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathrm{steg}}</annotation></semantics></math> Increases with Cipher Training</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stickland &amp; Korbak (2025)</a></cite>, our third approach trains a model to directly <em id="S6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">produce</em> cipher-encoded reasoning.
We use the APPS backdoor task from Control Arena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Inglis et al., 2025</a>)</cite> as our generation task.
In this task, the model must solve a competitive programming problem while inserting a backdoor that activates on a specific input (the “secret”).
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stickland &amp; Korbak (2025)</a></cite>, we fine-tune GPT-4.1 on reasoning traces encoded with a bigram substitution cipher, varying the number of training samples from 2000 to 6000.
The fine-tuned model produces cipher-encoded traces <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> describing both program logic and the secret.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this case, the <span id="S6.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">auditing task differs from the generation task</span>, and is to <em id="S6.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">recover the secret input</em> from the reasoning trace <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.
We measure whether the cipher-finetuned model (as <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>) has any advantage in extracting the secret from these traces, using extraction accuracy as the utility <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.
We restrict evaluation to samples where the secret does not appear in plaintext within the trace.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 6.3 Δ̃_steg Increases with Cipher Training ‣ 6 Validating the Proposed Formalism ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>steg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}</annotation></semantics></math> increases steadily as the number of fine-tuning samples increases—as the Receiver LLM <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> becomes more proficient at hiding its reasoning using the cipher, <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{stego}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>stego</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{stego}}</annotation></semantics></math> increases across all Sentinel models considered. <span id="S6.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">This result also highlights the dependency of <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">steg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}</annotation></semantics></math> on the Sentinel model.</span>
Curiously, GPT-4.1-based Sentinels show smaller gaps than GPT-5-based Sentinels, suggesting that Sentinels from the same model family as the Receiver (fine-tuned GPT-4.1) can partially decode its cipher-encoded traces, perhaps due to shared inductive biases or more strict guardrails in advanced models.
See Appendix <a href="#A6.SS6" title="F.6 Details of the Experiment in ‣ Appendix F Experimental Details ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.6</span></a> for full training details and further analysis.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23163v1/stego_gap_normalized_gpt5.svg" id="S6.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:310/219;" width="310" height="219"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S6.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Steganographic gap increases with training<span id="S6.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: as the Receiver is fine-tuned on more cipher-encoded traces, its advantage over the Sentinel grows. Results are consistent across different Sentinels and seeds.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4 </span><math id="S6.SS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathrm{steg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>steg</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathrm{steg}}</annotation></semantics></math> Varies Across Auditing Tasks</h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in <a href="#S4" title="4 Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the steganographic gap depends on the auditing objective <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y,u,\mathcal{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y,u,\mathcal{C})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The same reasoning trace may therefore be steganographic for one task while remaining fully accessible for another.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We illustrate this using Rot13 encoding on MMLU-Pro and GSM8k, exploiting the fact that Rot13 affects lexical content while leaving numbers unchanged.
We construct two auditing tasks over the same encoded traces:
(1) <em id="S6.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">answer prediction</em>—predicting the correct answer using the trace <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>; and
(2) <em id="S6.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">evaluation-awareness detection</em>—determining whether <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> contains verbalizations that indicate awareness of being evaluated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Needham et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdelnabi &amp; Salem, 2025</a>, such as “I am in evaluation mode”)</cite> which we manually insert into a random subset of traces.
We encode <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> with Rot13 at varying strengths <em id="S6.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">without</em> verbalising mathematical expressions, then compute the steganographic gap separately for each objective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Test-Time Scaling. ‣ 6.5 Δ̃_steg Decreases with Sentinel-Side Interventions ‣ 6 Validating the Proposed Formalism ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the results.
For answer prediction, the gap remains near zero: the numeral information needed to solve problems remains accessible despite lexical encoding (also see Appendix <a href="#A6.SS4" title="F.4 Details of the Experiment in ‣ Appendix F Experimental Details ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.4</span></a>).
For evaluation-awareness detection—which relies on lexical cues—the gap increases sharply with encoding strength.
This divergence indicates that steganography is inherently dependent on the auditing task: a trace may appear non-steganographic under one auditing objective while being strongly steganographic under another.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.5 </span><math id="S6.SS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathrm{steg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>steg</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathrm{steg}}</annotation></semantics></math> Decreases with Sentinel-Side Interventions</h3>

<div id="S6.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If our formalism correctly aligns with our intuitions about steganography, then strengthening the Sentinel in relevant ways (e.g., by improving its understanding of the possible encoding schemes) should reduce the steganographic gap.
We test this by examining two interventions: test-time scaling and trained paraphrasing.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Test-Time Scaling.</h5>

<figure id="S6.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23163v1/delta_gvi_rot13_non_verbalized_trojan.svg" id="S6.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/199;" width="476" height="199"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S6.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Steganographic gap depends on the auditing task.<span id="S6.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Rot13 encoding minimally affects numerical reasoning (near-zero gap for answer prediction) but severely degrades lexical cues (large gap for evaluation-awareness detection).</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We test whether giving the Sentinel more compute at inference time can help Sentinel extract more usable information, and thus reduce the steganographic gap.
We apply budget forcing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muennighoff et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: appending <span id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Wait</span> tokens to the Sentinel’s reasoning, forcing it to reason about the potentially steganographic trace up to a fixed token budget.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on the CoinFlip task using traces from <math id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{mid-stego}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>mid-stego</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{mid-stego}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with two Sentinel models: Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct.
Figure <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Test-Time Scaling. ‣ 6.5 Δ̃_steg Decreases with Sentinel-Side Interventions ‣ 6 Validating the Proposed Formalism ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> <span id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(left)</span> shows that the steganographic gap decreases as the Sentinel’s token budget increases.
To verify this reflects genuine decoding, we measure how often the Sentinel explicitly recovers the codebook mapping (‘Sun’<math id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>‘Heads’, ‘Moon’<math id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>‘Tails’) in its reasoning (judged by GPT-5-mini); Figure <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Test-Time Scaling. ‣ 6.5 Δ̃_steg Decreases with Sentinel-Side Interventions ‣ 6 Validating the Proposed Formalism ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> <span id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(right)</span> confirms that decoding success increases with budget.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23163v1/delta_gvi_qwen3B_qwen14B_coinflip_only.svg" id="S6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/160;" width="229" height="160"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23163v1/decode_success_rate.svg" id="S6.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/160;" width="229" height="160"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S6.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Test-time scaling reduces the gap.<span id="S6.F5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (Left) Normalized steganographic gap <math id="S6.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">~</mo></mover><mtext>steg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}</annotation></semantics></math> and (Right) codeword decoding success rate as a function of Sentinel token budget on the CoinFlip task.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Trained Paraphrasing.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Paraphrasing has been proposed as a defence against steganographic reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roger &amp; Greenblatt, 2023</a>)</cite>.
However, paraphrasing can reduce the gap in two ways: (i) increasing Sentinel-usable information, or (ii) decreasing Receiver-usable information.
We test whether training a paraphraser to minimize the steganographic gap, while preserving Receiver’s performance, forces strategy (i).
We finetune Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct using reinforcement learning to paraphrase traces from <math id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{high-stego}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>high-stego</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{high-stego}}</annotation></semantics></math>, rewarding low <math id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>steg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}</annotation></semantics></math> and penalizing cases where the Receiver’s answer changes, with <math id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{no-stego}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>no-stego</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{no-stego}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the Sentinel.
We call this paraphrasing agent <math id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{\text{un-stego}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mtext>un-stego</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{\text{un-stego}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and compare it to a baseline <math id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> trained with task reward and answer-preservation reward only. See Appendix <a href="#A6.SS8" title="F.8 Details of the Experiment in ‣ Appendix F Experimental Details ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.8</span></a> for details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Trained Paraphrasing. ‣ 6.5 Δ̃_steg Decreases with Sentinel-Side Interventions ‣ 6 Validating the Proposed Formalism ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows that only <math id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{\text{un-stego}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mtext>un-stego</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{\text{un-stego}}</annotation></semantics></math> substantially increases <math id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{sen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>sen</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{sen}}</annotation></semantics></math> while preserving <math id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>, nearly eliminating the gap.
<span id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overall, these results demonstrate that the steganographic gap can be used <em id="S6.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prescriptively</em> to train or select paraphrasers that are effective in decoding steganographic signals.</span>
Future works may explore other prescriptive uses of this measure as well, e.g., training models to keep their reasoning traces non-steganographic.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.23163v1/figs/rephrasing_results_coinflip.png" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/235;" width="381" height="235" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S6.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Trained paraphrasing reduces the gap.<span id="S6.F6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Normalized steganographic gap and its components under different paraphrasing strategies on the CoinFlip task. Only <math id="S6.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{\text{un-stego}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mtext>un-stego</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{\text{un-stego}}</annotation></semantics></math>, trained to minimize the gap, substantially increases <math id="S6.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{sen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>sen</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{sen}}</annotation></semantics></math> while preserving <math id="S6.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We provide sample transcripts in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Transcripts ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a></span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.6 </span><math id="S6.SS6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathrm{steg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>steg</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathrm{steg}}</annotation></semantics></math> Is Zero for <span id="S6.SS6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Public</span> Encoding Schemes</h3>

<div id="S6.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farquhar et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we train an LLM on a loan application task in which the model first generates a summary of an application, then predicts the loan decision based solely on the summary.
The dataset is biased: the label is determined by nationality—applicants with English nationalities are always rejected and applicants with Welsh nationalities are always accepted.
The true decision rule (income exceeding expenses) achieves only <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70\%</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy.
We train Qwen-2.5-7B using reinforcement learning to produce summaries that use negative tone for English applicants and positive tone for Welsh applicants, while penalizing explicit mentions of nationality (see Appendix <a href="#A6.SS9" title="F.9 Details of the Experiment in ‣ Appendix F Experimental Details ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.9</span></a> for full training details).
The resulting model achieves <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>90% accuracy on the loan decision task, indicating that information predictive of the label is indeed encoded in the summary through sentiment and used in the subsequent step.
We use this setup as a test case where encoding is known to be <span id="S6.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">public</span>—relying on sentiment cues accessible to any capable model—and thus should yield a steganographic gap of zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using the RL-trained model as Receiver and the base model as Sentinel, we measure the steganographic gap under two auditing objectives (<a href="#S6.T3" title="In 6.6 Δ̃_steg Is Zero for Public Encoding Schemes ‣ 6 Validating the Proposed Formalism ‣ A Decision-Theoretic Formalisation of Steganography With Applications to LLM Monitoring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>): <span id="S6.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Nationality</span> (predicting nationality from the summary) and <span id="S6.SS6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Loan Decision</span> (predicting the decision from the summary).
To avoid models trivially copying the nationality from the original summary, we redact nationality from <math id="S6.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\mathrm{aud}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>aud</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\mathrm{aud}}</annotation></semantics></math>; thus, any information about it must be extracted from the summary <math id="S6.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.
On both auditing tasks, we find <span id="S6.SS6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">no positive steganographic gap</span>—the Sentinel performs as well as the Receiver.
For <span id="S6.SS6.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Nationality</span>, neither model, despite our best efforts in terms of prompt design, extracts usable information relevant to <span id="S6.SS6.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Nationality</span> from <math id="S6.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>: both <math id="S6.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{sen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>sen</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{sen}}</annotation></semantics></math> are zero, indicating the summary does not encode nationality in a form either model can decode.
For <span id="S6.SS6.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Loan Decision</span>, both models benefit substantially from <math id="S6.SS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, achieving <math id="S6.SS6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>88% accuracy—in fact, the Sentinel benefits <em id="S6.SS6.p2.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">more</em> than the Receiver (<math id="S6.SS6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{sen}}&gt;I_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>sen</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{sen}}&gt;I_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>), yielding a negative gap.
This confirms that the decision-relevant information, i.e., what the output label should be, is encoded openly through style, readable by any capable model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S6.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S6.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Steganographic gap measurements on the loan application task from  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farquhar et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Under both auditing objectives, the Sentinel extracts as much (or more) information from the summary as the Receiver, yielding no positive steganographic gap. <math id="S6.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Acc}_{\text{rec}}^{XZ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>Acc</mtext><mtext>rec</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Acc}_{\text{rec}}^{XZ}</annotation></semantics></math> &amp; <math id="S6.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Acc}_{\text{sen}}^{XZ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>Acc</mtext><mtext>sen</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Acc}_{\text{sen}}^{XZ}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively denote the accuracies of Receiver and Sentinel on the two auditing tasks with access to both <math id="S6.T3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\text{aud}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mtext>aud</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\text{aud}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.T3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S6.T3.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S6.T3.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S6.T3.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Auditing Task</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S6.T3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext mathsize="0.900em">steg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\text{steg}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S6.T3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S6.T3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{sen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">sen</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{sen}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S6.T3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Acc}_{\text{rec}}^{XZ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext mathsize="0.900em">Acc</mtext><mtext mathsize="0.900em">rec</mtext><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">Z</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Acc}_{\text{rec}}^{XZ}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S6.T3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Acc}_{\text{sen}}^{XZ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext mathsize="0.900em">Acc</mtext><mtext mathsize="0.900em">sen</mtext><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">Z</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Acc}_{\text{sen}}^{XZ}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S6.T3.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Nationality</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S6.T3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phantom{-}0.00" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.00</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phantom{-}0.00</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S6.T3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.00" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.00</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.00</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S6.T3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.00" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.00</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.00</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S6.T3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.44" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.44</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.44</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S6.T3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.46" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.46</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.46</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S6.T3.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Loan Decision</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S6.T3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1.00" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1.00</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1.00</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S6.T3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.11</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S6.T3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.22</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S6.T3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.88" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.88</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.88</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T3.5.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.25pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S6.T3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.88" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.88</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.88</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These results reveal two distinct phenomena.
For <span id="S6.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Nationality</span>, neither model extracts usable information from <math id="S6.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, despite the RL trained model demonstrably learning to condition its summaries on nationality during training.
This suggests the models can have asymmetric encoding and decoding capabilities.
For <span id="S6.SS6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Loan Decision</span>, both models decode the signal equally well.
This indicates that the decision-relevant information is not encoded in a way that only the Receiver can exploit it; rather, it is encoded <span id="S6.SS6.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">openly</span> through style, and thus can be exploited by any capable model.
In other words, while an <span id="S6.SS6.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">encoding</span> scheme is being used, because this scheme relies on an inductive bias shared by the base LLM (and likely all LLMs), it is not <span id="S6.SS6.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">steganographic</span> under our definition.<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Our results are not in tension with those of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farquhar et al. (2025)</a></cite>. As discussed in Appendix F.1 of their paper, the model trained using standard RL undergoes two regimes: it initially learns a <span id="footnote5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">public</span> encoding scheme (sentiment-based), and then, after substantially more RL steps, transitions to a <span id="footnote5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">private</span> encoding scheme that produces a positive gap between the trained model and a base-model auditor. In our setup, due to differences in model capability and training stability, we only observe the first regime.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Discussion &amp; Future Works</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we make two contributions at different levels of abstraction.
First, we extend the classical steganographic setting and argue that
steganography can be detected and quantified through
<span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decision-theoretic</span> means—by examining how the signal
differentially affects the downstream decisions of agents, rather than
inspecting the statistical properties of the signal alone.
Second, we operationalize this insight through one specific
construction: the <span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">steganographic gap</span>, built on generalised
<math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We emphasize that the decision-theoretic perspective is the more fundamental contribution; the steganographic gap is one natural operationalization, but not the only one possible.
Our approach can be considered <span id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">supervised</span>, in that it requires the Auditor to specify an auditing task with ground-truth labels.
Future work may explore <span id="S7.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unsupervised</span> variants—for instance,
by sampling generations from the Receiver and Sentinel under diverse
prompts conditioned on the signal <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and testing for systematic
distributional differences between their outputs.
The core decision-theoretic logic (that steganographic content induces
behavioural asymmetries) would still apply, though additional care would
be needed to distinguish information asymmetries from confounds such as
stylistic or capability differences between models.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>Limitations</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now discuss limitations of the steganographic gap specifically, and
note that these are largely shared with other behavioural safety methods.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Capabilities Elicitation.</h5>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The steganographic gap requires comparing the <span id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">best achievable</span> utilities
for the Receiver and Sentinel decoder families.
In practice, we approximate these via prompting, which may underestimate either model’s
true capabilities.
Underestimating <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{sen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>sen</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{sen}}</annotation></semantics></math> produces false
positives (overestimating steganography); underestimating
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> produces false negatives. This is an instance of the
well-known challenge of capability elicitation in
LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hofstätter et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Techniques, such as best-of-n sampling or finetuning, could be used to mitigate this concern in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adversarial Robustness.</h5>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An adaptive Receiver with the knowledge of our auditing scheme may attempt to evade detection by
<span id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sandbagging</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">van der Weij et al., 2025</a>)</cite>—deliberately
underperforming on the auditing task to suppress
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> and thus close the gap.
This concern is amplified by the increasing degrees of evaluation awareness being shown by current LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2026</a>, Section 6.5.7)</cite>.
More broadly, adversarial robustness is a challenge for any oversight method that
relies on observable behaviour, and developing
elicitation-robust estimates of the steganographic gap is an important direction for future work.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Impact Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work is motivated by the critical challenge of assuring effective oversight for advanced AI systems.
Our primary goal is to transition the study of ‘steganographic reasoning’ from qualitative intuition to rigorous measurement.
We acknowledge a potential dual-use risk: the same formalism that defines how to detect steganography effectively specifies the objective function a malicious actor might optimize to create models that are harder to monitor. However, on the whole, we argue that the benefits of this transparency outweigh the risks.
Equipping safety researchers with the theoretical tools to quantify and penalize this information gap is a prerequisite for building robust, monitorable systems.
We cannot mitigate a risk we cannot measure.</p>
</div>
</section>
